  "slug": "end-structural-df64",
  "summary": "Recent preprints pair histology-based models with molecular targets and test reproducibility across slide scanners and datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is grounding histology-based predictions in measurable reference quantities and testing whether they hold under acquisition or dataset shift. One preprint trains an ABMIL model on H&E whole-slide images from TCGA to predict RPPA DNA damage response protein signaling across five gynecologic and breast cancers, reporting rho 0.43. Another applies tempering fine-tuning and few-shot calibration to pathology foundation models, aiming to reduce slide-scanner dependence of digital biomarkers in HPV, MSI, prostate grading and breast tasks. The white blood cell pipeline addresses a different axis, reporting cross-dataset macro-F1 of 0.879 on WBCBench2026 with context-preserving crops. What is new is the joint attention to molecular grounding and to acquisition-level or dataset-level robustness. All three items are preprints, so the findings remain provisional.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
